WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.990
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:10.910
Today we are going to engage in the favorite
pastime of the computer graphics researcher,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.360
which is…well, this.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.230
And this.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.910
And believe it or not, all of this is simulated
through a learning-based technique.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:29.750
Earlier, we marveled at a paper that showed
that an AI can indeed learn to perform a fluid

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.110
simulation.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:36.969
And just one more paper down the line, the
simulations it was able to perform extended

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:44.129
to other fields, like structural mechanics,
incompressible fluid dynamics, and more.

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:51.649
And even better, it could even simulate shapes
and geometries that it had never seen before.

00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:56.649
So today, the question is not whether an AI
can learn physics.

00:00:56.649 --> 00:01:00.500
The question is how well can an AI learn physics?

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:06.110
Let’s try to answer that question by having
a look at our first experiment.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.570
Here is a traditional, handcrafted technique,
and the new, neural network-based physics

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.790
simulator.

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:17.020
Both are doing fine so nothing to see he…whoa!

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.580
What happened?

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:25.010
Well, dear Fellow Scholars, this is when a
simulation blows up.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:32.110
But the new one is still running, even when
some traditional simulators blow up.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.110
That is excellent.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:39.390
But, we don’t have to bend over backwards
to find other situations where the new technique

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.700
is better than the previous ones.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.860
You see the reference simulation here, and
it is all well and good that the new method

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.410
does not blow up, but how accurate is it on
this challenging scene?

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.180
Let’s have a look.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:00.270
The reference shows a large amount of bending,
where the head is roughly in line with the

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.270
knees.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.600
Let’s memorize that.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.550
Head in line with the knees.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:05.660
Got it.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:11.450
Let’s see how the previous methods were
able to deal with this challenging simulation.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:18.230
When simulating a system of smaller size…well,
none of these are too promising.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:24.350
When we crank up the simulation domain size,
the physical modal derivative, PMD in short

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.280
does pretty well.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.980
So, what about the new method?

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.840
Both bend quite well.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.420
Not quite perfect, remember, the head would
have to go down to be almost in line with

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:38.560
the knees.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.130
But, amazing progress nonetheless.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:46.810
This was a really challenging scene, and,
in other cases, the new method is able to

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:50.680
match the reference simulator perfectly.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:58.680
So far this sounds pretty good, but PMD seems
to be a contender, and that, dear Fellow Scholars,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.710
is a paper from 2005.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.860
From 16 years ago!

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.800
So why showcase this new work?

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.900
Well, we have forgotten about one important
thing.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.760
And here comes the key.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:19.850
The new simulation technique runs from 30
to almost 60 times faster than previous methods.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:20.850
Whoa.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.780
How is that even possible?

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.320
Well, this is a neural network-based technique.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.540
And training a neural network typically takes
a long time, but, we only need to do this

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:37.620
once, and when we are done, querying this
neural network typically can be done very

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:38.620
quickly.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.290
Does this mean…yes!

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.610
Yes it does.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:49.640
All this runs in real time for these dinosaur,
bunny and armadillo scenes, all of which are

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.250
built from about ten thousand triangles!

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:57.240
And, we can play with them by using our mouse
on our home computer.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:05.280
The cactus and hairball scenes require simulating
not tens, but hundreds of thousands of triangles,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.700
so these took a bit longer as they are running
between one and a half and two and a half

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.330
frames per second.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:18.600
So, this is not only more accurate than previous
techniques, not only more resilient than the

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:25.700
previous techniques, but is also 30 to 60
timers faster at the same time.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.020
Wow.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:33.480
And just think about the fact that just a
year ago, an AI could only perform low-resolution

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:39.280
fluid simulations, then, a few months ago,
more kinds of simulations.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.940
And then, today, just one more paper down
the line, simulations of this complexity.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:51.130
And just imagine what we will be able to do
just two more papers down the line!

00:04:51.130 --> 00:05:36.770
What a time to be alive!

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:45.100
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

